  "narrative": {
    "intro": "General Dynamics Information Technology obligated $206M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date, spread across 62 awards with an average award size of $3.3M. Obligations fell 95.8% year over year. Annual context shows much larger prior years: FY22 obligations were $4.1B, FY23 were $4.3B, FY24 were $4.8B, FY25 were $4.6B, and FY26 were $4.9B. The FY2027 total is a partial, fiscal year to date figure and represents a sharp reduction from the prior fiscal year totals. Top buying agencies in FY2027 to date are the Department of Commerce with $61M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $52.6M, followed by the Department of the Interior with $38.7M and the Department of Transportation with $30.8M. Top markets by NAICS in FY2027 to date are Computer Systems Design Services with $87.7M and Other Computer Related Services with $60.5M. This snapshot highlights where current-year federal spending for General Dynamics Information Technology is concentrated and how it compares to recent full fiscal-year levels.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why are FY2027 obligations much lower than prior years?",
        "body": "General Dynamics Information Technology obligated $206M in FY2027 to date, down 95.8% year over year. Prior full fiscal years show obligations between $4.1B and $4.9B, so the current-year total is a partial figure and not comparable to full-year totals. The drop indicates fewer or smaller awards to date rather than a guaranteed annual trend. Watch award flow and timing for the rest of the fiscal year to see whether obligations pick up."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is FY2027 spending concentrated?",
        "body": "Department of Commerce is the largest buyer in FY2027 to date with $61M, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services with $52.6M and the Department of the Interior with $38.7M. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest NAICS market with $87.7M, and Other Computer Related Services follows with $60.5M. These agencies and markets account for most of the FY2027 to date obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
